  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 257 PM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nDeep ridging over the central CONUS will be the predominant driver \nof our upcoming weather over the next several days. Warm SW flow has \npushed temperatures well above normal, with high temperatures in the \nlow to mid 90s through Thursday. This in combination with dew points \nin the low 70s will push heat index values above 100 each afternoon, \nprompting a Heat Advisories until 8PM Thursday.\n\nThe main source of uncertainty will be the amplitude of the \naforementioned ridge over the lower Great Lakes and Ohio Valley.\nDiabatic processes from upstream convection and upper level water \nvapor will mitigate pressure rises a bit, keeping the apex of the \nridge just north of central Indiana. Confidence in convection on the \nnorthern periphery of the ridge is high, but how far south this area \nof convection reaches is where confidence wanes. Currently the \nexpectation is for a majority of any precipitation tomorrow through \nThursday afternoon to remain north of the area, but the margin for \nerror is small. Any rainfall and associated cloud cover that does \noccur will limit diurnal temperature gains, thus temperature \ngradients across the state could be significant. \n\nWithin the super-geostrphic upper level flow, a short wave is \nexpected to develop late tomorrow through Thursday. As this \nshortwave dives south into the east Great Lakes, modest lift along \nthe leading edge will likely lead to an area of more organized \nconvection late Thursday through Thursday night. Ensemble spread on \nwhere this convection will focus is still high, but generally most \nmembers agree on an area from northeast IN through northern OH. The \nsevere threat will be greatest over this area, but parameter spacing \nis sufficient for damaging wind gusts over central Indiana if enough \nlift is able reach this far west and initiate convection. \n\nDepending on where diabatic pressure chances occur with the \npreviously mentioned MCS, a weak surface trough may develop near or \nwithin central Indiana Thursday night. This area of troughing will \nlikely lead to additional rain and storm chances, but confidence on \nlocation is low at this time. A deepening trough over the East \nCoast, will push the ridge westward towards the end of the week, \nleading to increased uncertainty this weekend onward as the ridge \naxis falls west of the area and potential shortwaves increase \nchances for rain.",
